faqs:
  - question: "How do I return a native response from an Appwrite Function?"
    answer: "Return the response type of your runtime's web server instead of calling a `context.res` method: a Fetch API `Response` in Node.js 18 and later, Bun, and Deno, an aiohttp `web.Response` in Python, or a shelf `Response` in Dart. Appwrite sends its status, headers, and body to the client. See [Return a response](/docs/products/functions/develop#native-response)."
  - question: "How do I get the native request in an Appwrite Function?"
    answer: "Read `context.req.raw`. It's a Fetch API `Request` in Node.js, Bun, and Deno, an aiohttp `web.Request` in Python, a shelf `Request` in Dart, and an `*http.Request` in Go. The other runtimes pass their own server's request type. The parsed fields on `context.req` keep working after you read its body. See [Read the request](/docs/products/functions/develop#native-request)."
  - question: "Which runtimes support native request and response?"
    answer: "Every runtime has `context.req.raw` except the deprecated `node-14.5`, `node-16.0`, `dart-2.15`, and `swift-5.5` runtimes. Native responses work in Node.js 18 and later, Bun, Deno, Python, and Dart. Functions in PHP, Ruby, Go, .NET, Kotlin, Java, Swift, C++, and Rust answer through the `context.res` methods."
  - question: "Can a Python function stream a native response?"
    answer: "No. The Python runtime accepts an aiohttp `web.Response` with an in-memory body, and returning a `web.StreamResponse` or `web.FileResponse` fails the execution. Use `context.res.sendStream()` to stream from Python. Native responses do stream in Node.js, Bun, Deno, and Dart."
  - question: "Do I have to change my existing functions?"
    answer: "No. `context.req` and the `context.res` methods work exactly as before. One function can also mix both, for example by reading a form from `context.req.raw` and answering with `context.res.json()`."
  - question: "Can I run Hono in an Appwrite Function?"
    answer: "Yes. Hono's `app.fetch()` takes a `Request` and returns a `Response`, so a function that exports `async ({ req }) => app.fetch(req.raw)` runs a Hono app. Any other framework that exposes its app as a function from `Request` to `Response` works the same way."
  - question: "How do I use log() and the Appwrite API key inside Hono routes?"
    answer: "Appwrite headers such as `x-appwrite-key` and `x-appwrite-user-id` are on the request, so a route reads them with `c.req.header()`. To log, pass the function context to Hono with `app.fetch(req.raw, context)` and call `c.env.log()` inside a route."
---

Every Appwrite Functions runtime runs your code inside a web server, and each of those servers has its own request and response types. The JavaScript runtimes use the Fetch API's [`Request`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Request) and [`Response`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response), the same classes behind `Bun.serve()`, `Deno.serve()`, Cloudflare Workers, and frameworks such as Hono. Python runs on [aiohttp](https://docs.aiohttp.org), Dart on [shelf](https://pub.dev/packages/shelf), Go on `net/http`, and .NET on ASP.NET Core.

Until now, your function never saw those objects. `context.req` held a parsed copy of the request, and you built the answer with `context.res.text()` or `context.res.json()`. That works for a single endpoint, but code written for the server's own types had to be converted to `context.req` and `context.res` before it could run in a function. Even reading a multipart form in Node.js meant wrapping `req.bodyBinary` in a temporary `Response` just to call `formData()` on it.

Today, we are announcing **native request and response support in Appwrite Functions**. Every runtime now passes its web server's request to your function as `context.req.raw`. In Node.js 18 and later, Bun, Deno, Python, and Dart, your function can also return a native response instead of calling a `context.res` method.

This matters for two reasons. Functions now work as a compatibility layer for the frameworks and libraries already built on these types, so code that takes a `Request` or returns a `Response` runs in a function as written. It also makes functions easier for AI agents to write. Agents already know the Fetch API, aiohttp, and shelf, so they can build an Appwrite Function with the request and response types they've seen many times before, instead of first learning the details of the `context` object.

# What native request and response give you

- **The server's request in every runtime.** `context.req.raw` is a Fetch API `Request` in Node.js, Bun, and Deno, an aiohttp `web.Request` in Python, a shelf `Request` in Dart, and an `*http.Request` in Go. The other runtimes pass their server's request type too.
- **Standard body parsers in JavaScript.** `formData()`, `json()`, `text()`, `arrayBuffer()`, and `blob()` all work on `context.req.raw`, including for multipart uploads.
- **Native responses in five runtimes.** Return a Fetch API `Response` in Node.js, Bun, or Deno, an aiohttp `web.Response` in Python, or a shelf `Response` in Dart, and Appwrite sends it to the client as written.
- **Responses built by other libraries.** If a JavaScript library returns a `Response`, your function can return it as is. A `ReadableStream` body is streamed to the client, the same way as [`res.sendStream()`](/blog/post/announcing-streaming-responses).
- **Compatibility with Fetch API frameworks.** A framework whose app takes a `Request` and returns a `Response`, such as Hono, runs in a function when you pass it `context.req.raw`.
- **Nothing to migrate.** `context.req` and `context.res` work as before, and a function can use both.

# Use Request and Response in Node.js, Bun, and Deno

This function takes the `Request` from `context.req.raw` and answers with a `Response`. Everything after the first line uses only the Fetch API built into the runtime, with no Appwrite helpers.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
export default async ({ req }) => {
    const request = req.raw;

    if (request.method !== 'POST') {
        return new Response('Send the form with POST', { status: 405 });
    }

    const form = await request.formData();
    const name = form.get('name');

    return new Response(`Hello, ${name}!`, {
        headers: { 'content-type': 'text/plain' }
    });
};
```
```bun
export default async ({ req }) => {
    const request = req.raw;

    if (request.method !== 'POST') {
        return new Response('Send the form with POST', { status: 405 });
    }

    const form = await request.formData();
    const name = form.get('name');

    return new Response(`Hello, ${name}!`, {
        headers: { 'content-type': 'text/plain' }
    });
};
```
```deno
export default async ({ req }: any) => {
    const request: Request = req.raw;

    if (request.method !== "POST") {
        return new Response("Send the form with POST", { status: 405 });
    }

    const form = await request.formData();
    const name = form.get("name");

    return new Response(`Hello, ${name}!`, {
        headers: { "content-type": "text/plain" }
    });
};
```
{% /multicode %}

`context.req.raw` has the same URL, method, headers, and body as `context.req`, including Appwrite headers such as `x-appwrite-key` and `x-appwrite-user-id`. You can read its body once, like any `Request`, and `req.bodyText`, `req.bodyJson`, and `req.bodyBinary` keep working after you do. `formData()` parses URL-encoded HTML forms and multipart uploads, and `form.get('file')` returns a `File` with its name, type, and contents.

Until now, reading a multipart body meant building a `Response` around the raw bytes, as the [email trigger](/docs/products/functions/email) examples do. With `context.req.raw`, it's one call:

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #form-after title="After" %}

```server-nodejs
const form = await req.raw.formData();
```

{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #form-before title="Before" %}

```server-nodejs
const form = await new Response(req.bodyBinary, {
    headers: { 'content-type': req.headers['content-type'] }
}).formData();
```

{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

# Return the Response a library builds

Some libraries finish their work by building a `Response`. The [Vercel AI SDK](https://ai-sdk.dev) is one. In the [streaming announcement](/blog/post/announcing-streaming-responses), we passed its text stream to `res.sendStream()`. Now the function can read the prompt from `context.req.raw` and return the SDK's own response:

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
import { streamText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

export default async ({ req }) => {
    const { prompt } = await req.raw.json();

    const result = streamText({
        model: openai('gpt-5-mini'),
        prompt
    });

    return result.toTextStreamResponse();
};
```
```bun
import { streamText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

export default async ({ req }) => {
    const { prompt } = await req.raw.json();

    const result = streamText({
        model: openai('gpt-5-mini'),
        prompt
    });

    return result.toTextStreamResponse();
};
```
```deno
import { streamText } from "npm:ai";
import { openai } from "npm:@ai-sdk/openai";

export default async ({ req }: any) => {
    const { prompt } = await req.raw.json();

    const result = streamText({
        model: openai("gpt-5-mini"),
        prompt
    });

    return result.toTextStreamResponse();
};
```
{% /multicode %}

Its body is a `ReadableStream`, so the reply reaches the browser as the model writes it. The same streaming rules apply as for `sendStream()`. Clients see the chunks when they call the function through its domain, and the stream ends at the function's timeout or after 60 seconds, whichever comes first.

# Run Fetch API frameworks such as Hono

Native request and response also make Functions compatible with many web frameworks. A framework built on the Fetch API exposes your app as a single function that takes a `Request` and returns a `Response`. That's the same shape `Bun.serve()` and `Deno.serve()` call. Until now, running one of these frameworks in a function meant converting `context.req` into a `Request` yourself and copying the `Response` back into a `context.res` call. Now you pass `context.req.raw` in and return what comes out:

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #framework-after title="After" %}

```server-nodejs
export default async ({ req }) => app.fetch(req.raw);
```

{% /tabsitem %}

{% tabsitem #framework-before title="Before" %}

```server-nodejs
export default async ({ req, res }) => {
    const request = new Request(req.url, {
        method: req.method,
        headers: req.headers,
        body: ['GET', 'HEAD'].includes(req.method) ? undefined : req.bodyBinary
    });

    const response = await app.fetch(request);
    const body = Buffer.from(await response.arrayBuffer());

    return res.binary(body, response.status, Object.fromEntries(response.headers));
};
```

{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

[Hono](https://hono.dev) is one of these frameworks. Its `app.fetch()` method is that single function, so the last line of this function is all it takes to run a Hono app:

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
import { Hono } from 'hono';

const app = new Hono();

app.get('/', (c) => c.text('Hello from Hono!'));

app.get('/me', (c) => {
    const userId = c.req.header('x-appwrite-user-id');
    if (!userId) {
        return c.json({ message: 'Sign in first' }, 401);
    }

    return c.json({ userId });
});

export default async ({ req }) => app.fetch(req.raw);
```
```bun
import { Hono } from 'hono';

const app = new Hono();

app.get('/', (c) => c.text('Hello from Hono!'));

app.get('/me', (c) => {
    const userId = c.req.header('x-appwrite-user-id');
    if (!userId) {
        return c.json({ message: 'Sign in first' }, 401);
    }

    return c.json({ userId });
});

export default async ({ req }) => app.fetch(req.raw);
```
```deno
import { Hono } from "npm:hono";

const app = new Hono();

app.get("/", (c) => c.text("Hello from Hono!"));

app.get("/me", (c) => {
    const userId = c.req.header("x-appwrite-user-id");
    if (!userId) {
        return c.json({ message: "Sign in first" }, 401);
    }

    return c.json({ userId });
});

export default async ({ req }: any) => app.fetch(req.raw);
```
{% /multicode %}

Add `hono` to your `package.json` in Node.js and Bun. Deno imports it with an `npm:` specifier. A few details make this fit the rest of Appwrite Functions:

- **Routes match the request path.** A request to the function's [domain](/docs/products/functions/domains) uses the path in its URL. An [execution](/docs/products/functions/execute) created from an SDK uses the path you pass when you create it, so a signed-in user can call `/me` from your app.
- **Appwrite headers come along.** `x-appwrite-key`, `x-appwrite-user-id`, `x-appwrite-trigger`, and the other [function headers](/docs/products/functions/develop#headers) are on the `Request`, so routes read them with `c.req.header()`.
- **Logging still works.** Pass the function context as Hono's environment with `app.fetch(req.raw, context)`, then call `c.env.log()` or `c.env.error()` inside a route.

Any other framework that exposes its app as a function from `Request` to `Response` works the same way.

# Return native responses from Python and Dart

Python and Dart functions can return the response type of their runtime's server too. In Python, that's an aiohttp [`web.Response`](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_reference.html#aiohttp.web.Response), including the ones built by helpers such as `web.json_response()`. In Dart, it's a shelf [`Response`](https://pub.dev/documentation/shelf/latest/shelf/Response-class.html).

This function answers with JSON and sets a cookie. In Python, aiohttp's `set_cookie()` writes the `set-cookie` header and its attributes for you.

{% multicode %}
```python
from aiohttp import web

def main(context):
    response = web.json_response({"theme": "dark"})
    response.set_cookie("theme", "dark", max_age=3600, httponly=True, secure=True)

    return response
```
```dart
import 'dart:async';
import 'dart:convert';

import 'package:shelf/shelf.dart' as shelf;

Future<dynamic> main(final context) async {
    return shelf.Response.ok(
        jsonEncode({'theme': 'dark'}),
        headers: {
            'content-type': 'application/json',
            'set-cookie': 'theme=dark; Max-Age=3600; Path=/; HttpOnly; Secure'
        },
    );
}
```
{% /multicode %}

A few details differ between the two:

- **Python already includes aiohttp.** There's nothing to add to `requirements.txt`. In Dart, add `shelf` to your `pubspec.yaml`.
- **Python responses need an in-memory body.** Returning a `web.StreamResponse` or `web.FileResponse` fails the execution, so keep using `context.res.sendStream()` to stream from Python.
- **Dart responses can stream.** A shelf `Response` with a `Stream` body is sent in chunks, the same way as `sendStream()`.
- **Each cookie keeps its own header.** Python and Dart send every `set-cookie` header separately, so browsers store all of them.

# Read the native request in every runtime

`context.req.raw` works in every runtime, not only the ones that accept native responses. Each runtime passes the request object of its own web server, so you can use that server's helpers or hand the request to a library written for it.

| Runtime                | `context.req.raw` is       |
|------------------------|----------------------------|
| Node.js 18+, Bun, Deno | Fetch API `Request`        |
| Python                 | aiohttp `web.Request`      |
| Dart                   | shelf `Request`            |
| PHP                    | `Swoole\Http\Request`      |
| Ruby                   | `Sinatra::Request`         |
| Go                     | `*http.Request`            |
| .NET                   | ASP.NET Core `HttpRequest` |
| Kotlin, Java           | Javalin `Context`          |
| Swift                  | Vapor `Request`            |
| C++                    | Drogon `HttpRequestPtr`    |
| Rust                   | `http::Request<Vec<u8>>`   |

Outside JavaScript, keep three details in mind:

- **The body is readable again.** The runtime reads the body before your function runs, then rewinds or buffers it, so you can read it through `raw` as well. In .NET, set `Context.Req.Raw.Body.Position` to `0` first.
- **Python can't parse multipart bodies from `raw`.** `await context.req.raw.post()` handles URL-encoded forms, but fails on multipart bodies because the runtime has already read them.
- **Go and Rust need newer type packages.** Go needs `github.com/open-runtimes/types-for-go/v4` `v4.0.9` or later, and Rust needs `openruntimes-types-for-rust` `1.0.3` or later.

The [native request docs](/docs/products/functions/develop#native-request) show how to read `raw` in all 14 languages.

# When to use native request and response

Reach for `context.req.raw` and native responses when:

- You need `formData()` or another standard body parser in JavaScript.
- A library you use takes your runtime's request type or returns its response type.
- You're moving a handler from `Bun.serve()`, `Deno.serve()`, Cloudflare Workers, aiohttp, or shelf and want to keep its request in, response out shape.
- One JavaScript function serves several routes and you want a framework such as Hono instead of a hand-written `switch` on `req.path`.

Stay with `context.req` and `context.res` when the function is a single endpoint. `req.bodyJson` and `res.json()` are shorter than parsing and building the objects yourself. The `context.res` methods also look the same in every Appwrite runtime, which helps if your team writes functions in more than one language.

One limit to know about. If a returned `Response` in Node.js sets more than one cookie, use `node-20.0` or later. Earlier Node.js versions join the cookies into a single `set-cookie` header, and browsers don't split it back into separate cookies. Bun, Deno, Python, and Dart keep them separate.

# Getting started with native request and response

Native request and response support is available on Appwrite Cloud today. `context.req.raw` works in every runtime except the deprecated `node-14.5`, `node-16.0`, `dart-2.15`, and `swift-5.5`, and native responses work in Node.js 18 and later, Bun, Deno, Python, and Dart. Read the request from `req.raw` when your server's own type helps, and return a native response when that's simpler than a `context.res` method.

